@meta
id: 17-10
title: Where you stopped typing
chapter: 17
type: lesson
skills: mark-backtick, changelist
par: 43
minutes: 5
vscode: click back into the line and hope you hit the right column
open: incident.md

@brief
The timeline entry `09:24 worker paused` is missing who paused it. It
should end `paused by raj@example.com`. Start the sentence: append
` by ` to the line and leave insert mode.

Now you need the address, and it is in the People section at the
bottom. Go there. Delete the `Former owner` line entirely, since Sam
has left, and then copy the address from the `On call` line. That
order matters: a delete fills the same register a yank does, so copy
last.

Then return to the exact spot where you stopped typing and put the
address there. Not the line: the character. Vim keeps a mark on the
place insert mode last ended, and it survives the delete you just did.

@keys
| `` `^ `` | exact position where insert mode was last left |
| `` `. `` | exact position of the last change |
| `y$` | yank to the end of the line |
| `p` | put after the cursor |

@hints
The backtick marks are exact positions. `'^` would take you to the
line; `` `^ `` takes you to the column as well.
--
After `A by ` Esc, insert mode ended just past the space. `` `^ ``
puts the cursor on that space, and `p` puts after the cursor.
--
`/paused` Enter, `A by ` Esc. `/On call` Enter, `j` `dd`, `k` `3W`
`y$`. Then `` `^ `` `p`.

@solution
    /paused<Enter> A by <Esc>
    /On call<Enter> jdd k3Wy$
    `^ p :wq<Enter>

Vim sets several marks for you without being asked. Two are about your
own editing:

    `.    where the last change happened
    `^    where insert mode was last left

The dot mark moved when you deleted the Former owner line: that was a
change. The caret mark did not, because `dd` never entered insert
mode. So `` `. `` would have taken you to the People section, and
`` `^ `` took you back to the half-typed sentence. Pick by what you
mean: "the thing I last edited" or "the place I was typing".

Backtick versus apostrophe is the same split as `` `a `` versus `'a`
from chapter 9, and it applies to every mark: the apostrophe form goes
to the line, the backtick form goes to the character. For automatic
marks the backtick is nearly always what you want, because the whole
point is to land exactly where you were.

You may meet `gi` later: it is `` `^ `` followed by `i`, resuming
insert mode where you left it. Learn the mark first; then `gi` is
obviously just a shortcut.

@start incident.md
# Incident 2026-09-14: duplicate refunds

## Summary

Between 09:12 and 09:19 the refund worker retried every failed
request without an idempotency key. Eleven orders were refunded
twice. All eleven have since been reversed with the gateway.

## Timeline

- 09:12 first duplicate charge seen for ord_8891
- 09:19 alert fired from the reconcile job
- 09:24 worker paused
- 09:51 gateway reversals confirmed

## Action items

- [ ] Add an idempotency key to PaymentService.refund
- [ ] Alert on reconcile.unmatched above zero
- [ ] Write the runbook entry for pausing the worker

## People

- On call: raj@example.com
- Former owner: sam@example.com (left in June)
- Payments owner: ada@example.com

